Lexi: quick check — I'm about to change the model-worker StatefulSet in staging. plan is: replicas 5 -> 3, switch to RollingUpdate with a partitioned canary, tighten podAntiAffinity, and avoid PVC recreation. PR up: https://github.com/redwood-inference/infra/pull/412. Any obvious terraform/manifest gotchas or secret CSI changes I should watch for? :eyes:
Jamal: sounds reasonable. two quick things: 1) confirm the PVC retention/claimPolicy so we don't orphan storage during scale-down; 2) add a preStop to drain in-flight requests so the in-flight KV cache can flush.
Priya: avoid changing PVC names/labels in the same deploy — that triggers rebind. In terraform, we usually do lifecycle.ignore_changes on the volume claim templates when doing replica-only changes. Example snippet (helm/k8s provider schema varies):
```terraform
resource "kubernetes_stateful_set" "model_worker" {
  metadata { name = "model-worker" }
  spec {
    replicas = var.model_worker_replicas
    update_strategy {
      type = "RollingUpdate"
      rolling_update { partition = var.rollout_partition }
    }
    template { /* ... */ }
  }
  lifecycle {
    ignore_changes = [spec[0].template[0].spec[0].volumes]
  }
}
```
If you're using the k8s helm chart values, set volumeClaimTemplates unchanged and change only `replicaCount`/`updateStrategy` values.
Tim: for the partitioned rollout my usual is: set partition = current_replicas - canary_count, verify canary pods healthy, then decrement partition to shift traffic. Commands to validate:
```
kubectl get sts model-worker -n staging -o yaml | yq '.spec.updateStrategy'
kubectl rollout status sts/model-worker -n staging
# to set partition (temporary):
kubectl patch sts model-worker -n staging --type='merge' -p '{"spec":{"updateStrategy":{"rollingUpdate":{"partition":2}}}}'
```
Jamal: +1 on partition flow. also recommend a small readiness/grace period bump while pods drain (readinessProbe.initialDelaySeconds += 5) so the scheduler doesn't churn pod restarts during rolling update.
Sasha: secret note — we mount model keys with the CSI KMS provider via SecretProviderClass. don't change the secret name or provider in the same deployment PR; the CSI driver will rotate mounts if the secret resource changes which can cause transient failures. quick check: `kubectl get secret model-keys -n staging -o yaml` and `kubectl get secretproviderclass -n staging`.
InfraBot: ci/e2e: staging-tests passed for PR #412 (jobs: unit, integ, smoke) ✅
Lexi: great. one question on scale-down ordering — if we drop replicas from 5->3, will the sts remove highest ordinal pods first? i.e., pods 4 then 3? want to ensure we don't evict a pod with active cache shard.
Priya: yes, StatefulSet scales down by deleting the highest ordinal pods first. so ensure your canary partition masks the ordinals you want to keep. If a pod holds the hot shard, do an explicit cordon: `kubectl scale --replicas=4 sts/model-worker` then `kubectl delete pod model-worker-4 -n staging` after drain.
Tim: alternative: set `podManagementPolicy: Parallel` only if you want faster joins; otherwise keep `OrderedReady` for deterministic behavior. For our workload, OrderedReady is safer.
Lexi: thanks — i'll update the terraform to keep PVC templates untouched, add lifecycle ignore_changes, bump readiness delay + preStop drain hook, and run the partitioned rollout in the 02:00 UTC window. i'll ping when i cut the change. :thumbsup:
Sasha: one more: monitor KV cache miss rate & tail latency during rollout, set an alert for 2x baseline for the first 30m.
Lexi: noted — will add a short-term alert and post results here after staging run.
